/-!
# Elkik approximation of a generically smooth formal section

Source: Gabber and Ramero, Almost Ring Theory, Lemma 5.4.12, printed p.151,
specialized to the complement of the closed fibre; Elkik, Chapter II, Theorem 2 bis,
printed pp.566–567 (blueprint: lem-per-elkik-generic-smooth-section-approximation).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry

namespace Algebra

/-- A formal section of a finitely presented algebra smooth away from the closed fibre
can be approximated to every positive order over a Noetherian henselian pair.
The open in the hypothesis is exactly `Spec B` minus `V(IB)`, where `IB` is the
ideal generated by the image of `I`. No excellence or G-ring assumption is needed.

Source: Gabber and Ramero, Almost Ring Theory, Lemma 5.4.12, printed p.151;
Elkik, Chapter II, Theorem 2 bis, printed pp.566–567
(blueprint: lem-per-elkik-generic-smooth-section-approximation).

Proof (the node's induction on the number of generators of the henselian ideal):
1. Prove the stronger assertion for every finitely presented $A$-algebra and every formal section
whose punctured image lies in an open $W$ smooth over $A$; no smoothness away from $IA$ on the
entire algebra is required. Induct on the number $l$ of chosen generators of $I$, simultaneously
for all Noetherian henselian pairs. For $l=0$, $I=0$ and its completion is $A$, so use the formal
section itself. For $l=1$ use the principal-ideal approximation lemma with this $W$.

2. We record the henselian pairs used in the induction. Henselianity forces
$I\subseteq\operatorname{Jac}(A)$: otherwise for a maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ with $I+\mathfrak
m=A$, the distinct idempotents $0,1$ in the finite algebra $A/\mathfrak m$ have the same image in
its zero quotient by $I$, contradicting uniqueness. For any $K\subseteq I$, $(A/K,I(A/K))$ is
henselian by integral permanence. Also $(A,K)$ is henselian: for a finite $A$-algebra $E$ and an
idempotent $e\in E/KE$, reduce it modulo $IE$ and lift to an idempotent $f\in E$ by $(A,I)$. The
algebra $E$ is integral, so $IE$ is in its Jacobson radical. In $E/KE$, $e$ and the image of $f$
are equal by uniqueness modulo the radical ideal $IE/KE$; uniqueness of the lift follows the same
way. In particular $(A,tA)$ and $(A/t^MA,I(A/t^MA))$ are henselian for every $t\in I$ and
$M\geq1$.

3. For $l>1$ choose $I=(t,a_2,\ldots,a_l)$ and a presentation $B=A[x_1,\ldots,x_d]/J$. The rings
$A[x]$ and $B$ are Noetherian, hence the obstruction ideal $H=H_{B/A}$ and its inverse image
$\widetilde H\subseteq A[x]$ have finite generating lists. The smooth-locus assertion of ideal
calculus identifies $V(H)$ with the nonsmooth locus. The punctured-image hypothesis on $\widehat
s$ therefore implies $V(\widehat s(H)\widehat A)\subseteq V(I\widehat A)$. The
intersection-of-primes formula implies that each generator of $I\widehat A$ has some power in
$\widehat s(H)\widehat A$. If these powers have exponents $e_i\geq1$, every monomial of total
degree $h=1+\sum_i(e_i-1)$ contains one of them. Thus $(I\widehat A)^h\subseteq\widehat
s(H)\widehat A$.

4. Put $C=\widehat A_{(t)}$. This ring is Noetherian and $tC$-adically complete. Apply the
complete principal obstruction-lifting lemma to the base-changed presentation $C[x]/JC[x]$ and
this fixed $h$, obtaining $n_0,r$. Fix $L\geq\max(N,h+1)$ and then $M\geq\max(n_0,r+L,h+1)$. These
constants are chosen before taking an approximate point, as required by the lemma's uniform
quantifiers. Put $A_0=A/t^MA$ and $I_0=IA_0$. The ring $A_0$ is Noetherian (it is a finite type
quotient), is henselian along $I_0$ by Step 2, and $I_0$ defines the same adic topology as
$(a_2,\ldots,a_l)A_0$: $t^M=0$, so writing $J_0=(a_2,\ldots,a_l)A_0$, one has $J_0^k\subseteq
I_0^k$ and $I_0^k\subseteq J_0^{k-M+1}$ for $k\geq M$ by expanding monomials and discarding those
divisible by $t^M$. Thus the powers are cofinal. In particular the induction parameter decreases
to at most $l-1$.

5. Identify $D=\widehat{A_0}_{I_0}$ with $\widehat A_I/t^M\widehat A_I$. Indeed, complete the
finite-module exact sequence $0\to t^MA\to A\to A_0\to0$ along $I$. Exactness gives a surjection
$\widehat A_I\to D$. The finite-module tensor formula identifies the image of $(t^MA)^\wedge$ with
the ideal $t^M\widehat A_I$; the maps on representatives are multiplication and quotient, so this
is an isomorphism of rings commuting with every reduction. It follows that $D/I_0^LD$ is
canonically $A/(I^L+t^MA)$. As $M\geq L$ and $t\in I$, this last quotient is $A/I^L$. All
finite-module hypotheses hold because $A$ is Noetherian.

6. Reduce $\widehat s$ to a map $B_0=B\otimes_AA_0\to D$. Since $t$ is nilpotent in $A_0$,
$V(I_0D)=V(J_0D)$. Its punctured image therefore lies in the base change of the original smooth
open $W$, which is smooth by smooth base change (checked on affine opens). Thus the stronger
induction assertion, for the ideal generated by $a_2,\ldots,a_l$, supplies a map $s_0:B_0\to A_0$
agreeing with that reduced formal section modulo $I_0^L$. Powers of the two cofinal ideals give
the same completion; approximate to a sufficiently high power of $(a_2,\ldots,a_l)$ contained in
$I_0^L$. The needed henselianity for this smaller ideal was proved in Step 2. Finite presentation
of $B_0$ follows by applying coefficient reduction to the chosen finite equations.

7. Let $K_0\subseteq A_0$ be generated by evaluation under $s_0$ of the images of $H$. Congruence
of polynomial evaluations modulo $I_0^L$, and the power containment in Step 3 reduced to $D$, give
$I_0^hD\subseteq K_0D+I_0^LD$. The completion statements show that $D$ is Noetherian and
$I_0D$-adically complete. Apply Nakayama to the finite $D$-module $(I_0^hD+K_0D)/K_0D$: since
$L\geq h+1$, it equals its product with $I_0D$. This ideal is in the Jacobson radical because $D$
is adically complete. Hence $I_0^hD\subseteq K_0D$. The map $A_0\to D$ is faithfully flat, since
$I_0\subseteq\operatorname{Jac}(A_0)$ by Step 2. Ideal contraction now gives $I_0^h\subseteq K_0$
in $A_0$, in particular $t^h\bmod t^M\in K_0$. This is containment for the images of the original
$H$, not merely for a possibly larger obstruction ideal after base change.

8. Lift the generator values of $s_0$ arbitrarily to a tuple $a\in C^d$, using $C/t^MC=A/t^MA$.
Then $f(a)\in t^MC$ for every $f\in J$. Write $K_C(a)$ for evaluation of the inverse image of
$H_{B_C/C}$ in $C[x]$. Ideal calculus gives $H_{B/A}B_C\subseteq H_{B_C/C}$. Consequently Step 7
yields $t^h\in K_C(a)+t^MC$. Apply Nakayama to $((t^h)+K_C(a))/K_C(a)$, using $M>h$ and
$tC\subseteq\operatorname{Jac}(C)$, to remove the error $t^M$ and obtain $t^h\in K_C(a)$. The
complete lifting lemma at $n=M$ now gives $b\in C^d$ solving every relation and congruent to $a$
modulo $t^{M-r}$. It defines an $A$-algebra map $u:B\to C$.

9. The new section $u$ meets the original smooth locus away from $t$. To see this using precisely
the needed ideal, let $K_b$ be generated in $C$ by evaluations at $b$ of polynomial lifts of the
original $H$. Their evaluations at $a$ agree modulo $t^{M-r}$. Step 7 and $M-r\geq L>h$ imply
$t^h\in K_b+t^{M-r}C$. Nakayama on $((t^h)+K_b)/K_b$ gives $t^h\in K_b$. Every prime of $C$
avoiding $t$ thus avoids at least one image of $H$, so its image in $\operatorname{Spec}B$ is in
$W_1=\operatorname{Spec}B\setminus V(H)$. Ideal calculus identifies this open as the smooth locus.
By $(A,tA)$ henselian from Step 2, apply principal-ideal approximation with the smooth open $W_1$
to obtain $s:B\to A$ agreeing with $u$ modulo $t^L$.

10. Compare the maps in $\widehat A_I$. There is a canonical map $C\to\widehat A_I$: its $k$-th
coordinate is $C\to A/t^kA\to A/I^k$; these maps are compatible because $t^kA\subseteq I^k$. The
element $b_i$ and the value $s_0(x_i)$ agree modulo $t^{M-r}$, hence agree modulo $I^L$ under the
quotient identification of Step 5. The latter agrees with $\widehat s(x_i)$ modulo $I^L$ by Step
6. Finally $s(x_i)$ agrees with $b_i$ modulo $t^L\subseteq I^L$. Thus $q_Ls=\pi_L\widehat s$ on
generators and on $A$, hence on $B$. Reducing to order $N\leq L$ gives the claimed equality. This
completes the induction for the stronger assertion. For the item take
$W=\operatorname{Spec}B\setminus V(IB)$: the punctured-image condition is automatic for an
$A$-algebra map, since inverse image of $V(IB)$ is $V(I\widehat A)$.
-/
theorem exists_algHom_approximation_of_smooth_compl_zeroLocus
    {R B : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [Algebra R B] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) [HenselianRing R I] [FinitePresentation R B]
    (hsmooth : AlgebraicGeometry.Smooth
      (Scheme.Opens.ι (X := Spec (CommRingCat.of B))
        (⟨(PrimeSpectrum.zeroLocus (I.map (algebraMap R B) : Set B))ᶜ,
          (PrimeSpectrum.isClosed_zeroLocus _).isOpen_compl⟩ :
        (Spec (CommRingCat.of B)).Opens) ≫
          Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R B))))
    (shat : B →ₐ[R] AdicCompletion I R) (N : ℕ) (hN : 1 ≤ N) :
    ∃ s : B →ₐ[R] R,
      (AdicCompletion.evalₐ I N).comp shat = (Ideal.Quotient.mkₐ R (I ^ N)).comp s := by
  sorry

end Algebra
